      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the derivatives of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nx(t) = 2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny(t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left( 2 \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\left( -2 \\sin t \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\left( 2 \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\left( -2 \\sin t \\right)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t\n\\]\n\nFactor out the common factor of 4:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the integrand simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nIntegrate with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
